# Two environments, two files:
#
#   .env        production — lives on the server, consumed by docker-compose.yml
#   .env.local  development — your machine, git-ignored, loaded automatically by Next
#
# The values below are the PRODUCTION shape. Where development differs, it is
# called out inline. The rule worth keeping: a development environment must not
# write to a production resource. A dev setup where the database is a throwaway
# copy but storage and email are live is worse than no separation at all — it
# feels like a sandbox while the writes land in the real system.

# --- Database (existing self-hosted Postgres container, new DB/user created for this app) ---
# Development: point both at the local throwaway container instead —
#   postgresql://cvforge:devpassword@127.0.0.1:5432/cvforge
# See ARCHITECTURE.md → Local development database.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://cvforge:changeme@postgres:5432/cvforge
DIRECT_URL=postgresql://cvforge:changeme@postgres:5432/cvforge

# --- Auth (Better Auth) ---
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=                    # openssl rand -base64 32
BETTER_AUTH_URL=https://cv-forge.appfinningar.se

GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=                      # Google Cloud Console → OAuth 2.0 credentials
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# --- Email (Resend) ---
# NOT yet separated between environments: a key here sends real mail from the
# real domain, so testing a sign-up or a password reset locally delivers an
# actual message and counts against the production sender's reputation. Until
# auth.ts learns to log the link to the console outside production, either leave
# this blank in .env.local (sign-up then surfaces a real "email service not
# configured" error) or use a Resend key restricted to a test domain.
RESEND_API_KEY=                        # Resend dashboard → API Keys
EMAIL_FROM=CV Forge <noreply@appfinningar.se>

# --- AI (CV import from PDF) ---
GEMINI_API_KEY=                        # Google AI Studio

# --- File storage (Cloudflare R2, S3-compatible) ---
#
# Two buckets, both created with EU jurisdiction so uploaded photos are stored
# inside the EU:
#
#   cv-forge-bucket-eu    production   Custom Domain files.appfinningar.se
#   cv-forge-dev-bucket   development  no Custom Domain, not public
#
# S3_ENDPOINT is the same for both buckets, but note the `.eu.` segment: a bucket
# created with a jurisdiction is reachable ONLY through that jurisdiction's
# endpoint, and the plain <account-id>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com address will not
# see it at all. Both buckets here are EU, so both use the EU endpoint.
#
# Give each bucket its OWN API token, scoped in the token dialog to that single
# bucket ("Specify bucket(s)", not "All buckets"). The account holds unrelated
# buckets, and an all-buckets token reaches those too — including, from a dev
# machine, the production avatars.
#
# Create tokens at R2 → API → "Manage API tokens" — NOT the account-wide API
# Tokens page, which issues a bearer token with no S3 credentials at all. The
# screen shown right after creation is the only place the Access Key ID, the
# Secret Access Key, and the jurisdiction-specific endpoint ever appear
# together; Cloudflare will not show the secret again. Copy all three then. If
# you navigated away, the secret is unrecoverable — create a new token.
S3_ENDPOINT=https://<account-id>.eu.r2.cloudflarestorage.com
S3_BUCKET=cv-forge-bucket-eu
S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=                      # R2 API Token → Access Key ID
S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=                  # R2 API Token → Secret Access Key

# S3_PUBLIC_URL is the Custom Domain connected to the bucket
# (R2 bucket → Settings → Custom Domain). It is baked in at BUILD time, not read
# at runtime: next.config.ts derives images.remotePatterns from it, so the value
# is also passed as a --build-arg in .github/workflows/build-and-push.yml. If the
# two disagree, uploads succeed but Next/Image silently blocks the hostname and
# every avatar falls back to a placeholder.
S3_PUBLIC_URL=https://files.appfinningar.se

# Development storage — put these in .env.local instead of the three above:
#   S3_BUCKET=cv-forge-dev-bucket
#   S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID / S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY   from the dev bucket's own token
#
# The dev bucket has no Custom Domain, so there is no public URL to point at.
# Either leave a placeholder and accept that avatars do not render locally
# (they are stored and deleted correctly either way — only display breaks):
#   S3_PUBLIC_URL=http://localhost/not-public
# or enable R2 → dev bucket → Settings → Public Development URL and use the
# pub-<hash>.r2.dev address it hands you. That URL is rate-limited and unsuitable
# for production, which is exactly why it is safe to use here.
